---
title: "The pure layer"
description: "src/domain/: the three files that hold every decision worth testing, and why each one was pulled out of a handler."
---

`src/domain/` is 296 lines across three files, and it imports nothing but
`config.ts`. No Telegram, no database, no clock. Everything here is a function
from values to values, so all of it is unit tested normally.

The selection criterion is narrow: **a thing belongs here if it is a decision the
project would want to be sure about, and it can be stated without I/O.** Things
that merely happen to be pure but are trivial stay where they are used.

## `scoring.ts`

| Function | What it decides |
|---|---|
| `tierMinutes(tier)` | The config lookup. Minutes are always derived |
| `isTier(value)` | Type guard for untrusted callback payloads |
| `progressBar(done, total)` | The ten-slot bar, matching the mockup exactly |
| `isInWindow(date)` | FR-26. Plain string comparison on `yyyy-mm-dd` |
| `competitionRanks(values)` | Standard competition ranking, so ties read 1, 1, 3 |
| `previousWeek(weekStart)` | Steps one week back on a calendar label |
| `weeklyStreak(weeks, current)` | FR-13, the streak reduction |

Two are worth reading closely.

**`isInWindow` compares strings.** `"2026-09-21" >= "2026-07-27"` is a correct
comparison because ISO-8601 dates sort lexicographically. There is no date parsing
in it at all, which is the point: a parse would introduce a timezone, and a
timezone is where date bugs live.

**`weeklyStreak` handles the in-progress week specially.** If the current week has
not yet reached the target, the count starts from the *previous* week instead of
breaking. Without that, everyone's streak would read zero for most of every
Monday.

## `scheduling.ts`

This file exists because of one requirement, FR-20, and one property: the ticker's
most consequential decision must be testable without Telegram and without a clock.

`shouldPostMonday()` answers "is this chat owed a Monday post right now" from four
plain values. Three rulings are embedded in it:

1. **The condition is "no post yet for this week, and past Monday's hour", not
   "today is Monday".** A bot that was down for all of Monday posts on Tuesday.
   Over an eight-week competition, a missing post is worse than a late one, and
   the "today is Monday" version fails silently in exactly the case where
   something has already gone wrong.
2. **The previous week must overlap the competition at either end**
   (`previousWeekInCompetition`). On the first Monday there is no last week to
   report, and without this the first thing every guild chat would ever see is a
   winner announced at 0.0 minutes per member.
3. **`lastPosted` starts at the binding week**, so a chat bound on a Thursday is
   not immediately owed a "new week, back to zero" post.

::: {.callout-note}
## Why the boundary tests use the previous week, not this one

Both bounds in `previousWeekInCompetition` are tested against the **previous**
week rather than against `weekStart`, because the previous week is what the post
reports. That is what keeps them correct if the competition ever starts or ends
mid-week, and the two are exact mirrors of each other. See
`src/domain/scheduling.ts:41-72`.
:::

`isFinalMondayPost()` decides whether this is the competition's closing post,
which swaps the copy. It reads `weekStart`, **deliberately not** `isInWindow(today)`.
The two agree when the competition ends on a Sunday and diverge the moment it does
not: with an end on a Wednesday, the Thursday after is already outside the window
while that same week's Monday post is still an ordinary one with a partial week
still to come. Reading today's date there would print "That's the competition"
while the competition was still running.

## `reminders.ts`

`reminderAction()` is the whole of FR-21 and FR-22 as one pure function: given an
ignore count and whether the user has logged since the last ping, return what to
send and what the count becomes.

```ts
const effective = input.responded && input.ignoredStreak <= followupAfter
  ? 0
  : input.ignoredStreak;

if (effective >  followupAfter) return { action: "none",     nextStreak: effective };
if (effective === followupAfter) return { action: "followup", nextStreak: effective + 1 };
return { action: "daily", nextStreak: effective + 1 };
```

Read the first expression carefully, because it is the asymmetry the whole
requirement turns on. **A response clears the count only while the user is still
below the threshold.** Once the follow-up has gone out, logging does not resume
anything: the bot asked whether to keep messaging and got no answer, and logging is
engagement with the competition rather than consent to be messaged.

The header comment records why this file is unit tested end to end rather than
smoke tested: five consecutive ignores takes five days to reproduce by hand, so
`docs/SMOKE.md` cannot be its acceptance basis. That is also why it must not live
inside a Telegram-facing file.

`isWithinGrace()` is the second function here: whether now is still inside the
window in which a reminder for a given hour may be delivered. It cannot wrap past
midnight by construction, since `localHour` is 0 to 23, so hour 23 yields a window
of exactly `{23}`.

::: {.callout-important}
## This rule is stated twice, on purpose

`isWithinGrace` is the definition. `dueReminders()` in `src/db/reminders.ts`
applies the same rule again as a SQL `WHERE` clause, so the candidate set stays
small instead of loading every user into the process.

That duplication is deliberate and it is pinned: `tests/db/reminders.test.ts`
tests the two against each other at the edges. If you change one, the tests will
tell you about the other.
:::
